<Base title="Direct Primary Care: A Physician-Owned Growth Model" description="An independent operating-model proposal by Drew Cleaver, with an editable practice, launch and network worksheet for expert review." compact noindex>
 <article class="dpc-page" aria-labelledby="dpc-title">
  <header class="dpc-hero"><div class="dpc-shell">
   <p class="eyebrow">Independent concept by Drew Cleaver <span>Page revision {revision.pageRevision}</span></p>
   <p class="revision-stamp">Content finalized {updated}. Published {centralTimestamp(revision.publishedAt)}. <a href="#revision-history">Revision history</a></p>
   <h1 id="dpc-title">Direct Primary Care:<br /><span>A Physician-Owned<br />Growth Model</span></h1>
   <p class="hero-intro">Continuity with your own physician plus seven-day access to the physician team, with protected clinical time and a potential path to physician ownership. A working proposal for physicians, operators, advisors and potential investors to challenge and improve.</p>
   <div class="hero-actions"><a class="button paper" href="#concept">Read the concept ↓</a><a class="button" href="#model">Open the working model ↓</a><a class="button" href="/dpc-deck.pdf?v=R09" download="Direct-Primary-Care-Growth-Model.pdf">Download the 20-slide PDF ↓</a></div>
   <p class="disclosure">Independent and unaffiliated. No practice endorsement, operating results or committed participants are implied. Founder roles, ownership, compensation and partnerships remain undecided. This is an unlisted public page for expert review, not an investment offering.</p>
  </div></header>
  <nav class="section-nav dpc-shell" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#economics">Practice economics</a><a href="#launch">Opening and cash</a><a href="#ownership">Ownership</a><a href="#platform">Shared support</a><a href="#validation">Validation</a><a href="#model">Working model</a><a href="#save-model">Save and share feedback</a></nav>
  <div class="concept-reading">
   <section id="concept"><div class="reading-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">01 / The proposition</p><h2>A sustainable local practice is the foundation of the growth case.</h2>
    <p>The proposal starts with a familiar physician, dependable access and enough time to follow through. A small practice would pair a physician owner with up to two associates. Shared nonclinical services would handle work that practices can sensibly standardize, while physicians retain clinical judgment and responsibility for care.</p>
    <p>The growth hypothesis is that physicians could learn the operating model, demonstrate readiness and eventually own a practice. The support company would need to make that transition easier and provide continuing value worth its fee.</p>
    <div class="takeaway"><p><strong>Two economic tests must pass:</strong> patients and physicians must value the local practice, and practices must value the shared support enough to sustain its delivery costs. Neither result is established yet.</p></div>
   </div></section>
   <section id="category"><div class="reading-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">02 / Established category, proposed variation</p><h2>The premium price needs its own patient-value case.</h2>
    <p>DPC is an established model that charges periodic fees for primary care, generally outside traditional insurance billing. Physicians can be employees or owners. <a href="#source-aafp">AAFP category context</a></p>
    <div class="table-region" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Category comparison"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>Category context and hypotheses to test</caption><thead><tr><th>Established DPC context</th><th>This proposal</th></tr></thead><tbody>
     <tr><th scope="row">Recurring membership</th><td data-label="This proposal">Core $250 / Extended $350 per month</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Ongoing primary care access</th><td data-label="This proposal">Planned relationship visits and protected follow-up time</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Different practice ownership arrangements</th><td data-label="This proposal">One physician owner, up to two associates, and a potential ownership pathway</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Different operating arrangements</th><td data-label="This proposal">A shared support company with a defined service fee</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>AAFP’s overview describes a general $50–$100 monthly range. The proposed $250–$350 sits above that context and needs evidence of willingness to pay. This comparison is not a local market survey or proof that the service packages are equivalent. <a href="#source-aafp">Source</a></p>
    <div class="table-region"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>Demo trial targets per person. Proposed terms, not an active enrollment offer.</caption><thead><tr><th>Membership</th><th>Pay monthly</th><th>Pay annually upfront</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Core</th><td data-label="Pay monthly">$250 / month</td><td data-label="Pay annually upfront"><strong>$2,400 / year</strong><br />$200 / month equivalent · save $600</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Extended</th><td data-label="Pay monthly">$350 / month</td><td data-label="Pay annually upfront"><strong>$3,360 / year</strong><br />$280 / month equivalent · save $840</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p><strong>Paying a year upfront saves 20%.</strong> The discounted monthly equivalent is not a monthly billing option. Core visits use 25-minute bookings; Extended visits use 45-minute bookings. One routine visit per month with the assigned physician is included. Appointment access and payment inclusion are separate: whether short-notice encounters are included or use the existing extra-visit prices remains unresolved. The base assigns them no extra revenue; onboarding consumes time without an invented fee. Modeled cancellations refund unused prepaid months; final membership terms remain to be agreed.</p>
    <p>The calculator separates included routine attendance from extra paid visits and onboarding so utilization can be tested instead of hidden in one cap. Review the scope, access standard and pricing against local demand before fixing the offer. No part of this combination is claimed as a unique invention.</p>
   </div></section>
   <section class="green-band" id="experience"><div class="reading-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">03 / Proposed organization and care</p><h2>Five consecutive days each. Three shared onsite days.</h2>
    <p><strong>Associate A: Sunday–Thursday, with Friday and Saturday off. Associate B: Tuesday–Saturday, with Sunday and Monday off.</strong> Each works five consecutive onsite days. Both associates are onsite with the owner Tuesday–Thursday. This overlap is intentional for collaboration, mentorship and physician development. No teaching hours are assumed; protected mentoring must reduce bookable time when scheduled. The owner’s proposed day is Tuesday–Thursday, 10 a.m.–7 p.m.: three scheduled visits in 10–1, lunch in 1–2, three in 2–5, urgent reserve in 5–6, and messages/closeout in 6–7. This is six routine slots plus one held urgent encounter (seven total), separate from each associate’s cap. A third, junior associate is an optional Fri–Mon stretch scenario—not a baseline hire—if the model shows enough demand to justify added weekend and owner-off-day coverage.</p>
    <div class="schedule-guide"><div><span>Associate routine appointments</span><strong>9 a.m.–6 p.m.</strong><p>Visits finish by 6. Six hours remain for routine bookings after protected time.</p></div><div><span>Assigned physician continuity</span><strong>Any clinic day</strong><p>Routine visits stay with your physician. Other scheduled doctors provide team access for additional needs.</p></div><div><span>Total encounter ceiling</span><strong>Up to 12 / day</strong><p>Per associate, including urgent and onboarding encounters. Available time usually sets a lower limit.</p></div></div>
    <div class="table-region" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Proposed weekly coverage"><table class="coverage-table"><caption>Scheduled onsite days before leave. A: Sunday–Thursday; B: Tuesday–Saturday. Off means no assumed on-call duty.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">A</th><th scope="col">B</th><th scope="col">Owner</th><th scope="col">Total onsite</th></tr></thead><tbody>{mature.roster.daily.map(day=><tr><th scope="row">{day.day}</th>{[1,2,0].map(i=><td>{mature.roster.roles[i].weekdays[day.index]?'On':'Off'}</td>)}<td>{day.physicians}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>This is ten associate workdays spread across seven calendar days. The 46-week reference leaves six weeks per physician without scheduled relief. Sunday–Monday lose their sole scheduled physician if Associate A is away; Friday–Saturday do if Associate B is away. Coverage costs and an actual absence plan are still required.</p>
    <h3>The appointment window is narrower than the availability window.</h3>
    <p>The illustrative routine cutoff is <strong>6 p.m.</strong> Appointments must finish by then. A nine-hour booking window, with one hour each for breaks, messages/follow-up and urgent reserve, leaves six hours for routine appointments. At a 75% Extended mix, the 25/45-minute bookings average 40 minutes, or about nine routine visits per associate day; a Core-heavy mix can approach eleven.</p>
    <p>Seven-day coverage means a scheduled team doctor each day before leave. It does not mean every physician is available every day, continuous emergency coverage, a response-time guarantee or duties on days off. No late-evening commitment or owner escalation is assumed. If escalation is entered, the model tests owner reserve on the same day and flags off-day gaps.</p>
    <p>The 12 ceiling includes onboarding, urgent and visit-equivalent virtual encounters. Messages consume work time separately. A 25-minute Core or 45-minute Extended booking still needs room for documentation and follow-up. <a href="#source-ama">AMA context on work beyond appointments</a> supports measuring the full workload; it does not endorse this roster.</p>
    <p class="note">Core and Extended are modeled as 25- and 45-minute bookings tied to the $250/$350 tiers. New-patient onboarding is a separate 45-minute demand item; extra visits use the same 25/45-minute choices. Per-role block multipliers and routine attendance are editable. Routine care stays with the assigned physician on any of their clinic days. Team cross-coverage applies to additional and short-notice needs. Continuity, complexity and response quality require clinical review. Membership does not replace hospital or specialist coverage.</p>
   </div></section>
   <section id="economics"><div class="reading-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">04 / Mature economics under the revised basis</p><h2>Capacity follows assigned relationships and protected access.</h2>
    <dl class="key-figures"><div><dt>Members used for revenue</dt><dd>{mature.modeledMembers}</dd></div><div><dt>Annual revenue at that enrollment</dt><dd>{money(mature.revenue)}</dd></div><div><dt>Surplus after coverage and bonuses</dt><dd>{money(mature.surplus)}</dd></div></dl>
    <p><strong>Selected base: 100% monthly routine attendance.</strong> The concept encourages a recurring monthly relationship, so the base budgets every member’s included visit. The 70% comparison is not measured utilization. There are {number(mature.routineCapacity)} raw routine slots per month; the 85% routine scheduling target and replacement onboarding reduce the supported panel.</p>
    <p><strong>360 requested; {mature.modeledMembers} modeled; {mature.deferredMembers} beyond support.</strong> Routine visits distribute evenly across each assigned physician’s own clinic days. Editable panel weights start proportional to routine capacity. Uneven physician preference or clustered appointment times can reduce support. Monthly and weekday averages do not prove workable appointment-by-appointment scheduling.</p>
    <div class="table-region"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>Assigned panels at 100% attendance; no member belongs to more than one panel</caption><thead><tr><th>Physician</th><th>Raw slots / month</th><th>Routine panel limit</th><th>Modeled assigned members</th></tr></thead><tbody>{mature.panels.map(p=><tr><th>{p.name}</th><td data-label="Raw slots / month">{number(p.routineMonthly)}</td><td data-label="Routine panel limit">{p.panelLimit}</td><td data-label="Modeled assigned members">{p.members}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>The 15% routine buffer protects appointment variation and rescheduling after breaks, administration and explicit mentoring time have already been deducted. The short-notice reserve is separate and is not discounted again. Working weeks reflect leave once; they do not provide a substitute doctor. Scheduled mentoring must be entered as protected time before booking it.</p>
    <div class="table-region"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>Daily team access: monthly totals for each weekday, averaged over working weeks; 60-minute short-notice blocks</caption><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Doctors</th><th>Held encounters</th><th>Expected completed</th><th>Unused hours</th><th>Excess requests</th></tr></thead><tbody>{mature.demand.daily.map(d=><tr><th>{d.day}</th><td data-label="Doctors">{d.physicians}</td><td data-label="Held encounters">{number(d.reservedEncounters)}</td><td data-label="Expected completed">{number(d.expectedCompleted)}</td><td data-label="Unused hours">{number(d.unusedReserveMinutes/60)}</td><td data-label="Excess requests">{number(d.excessEncounters)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>One reserve hour per physician clinic day remains unavailable to routine bookings even when unused. Expected short-notice demand is five encounters per 100 members per month, with double weight on weekdays: an editable hypothesis. Sunday, Monday, Friday and Saturday have only one doctor; arrivals, acuity, leave and timing require operational validation before promising same-day/next-day service.</p>
    <p><strong>This is an arithmetic planning limit, not a clinically validated panel.</strong> At $325 blended list dues (25% Core / 75% Extended), 50% annual-prepay take-up at 20% off reduces earned dues to $292.50 per member per month; the modeled {mature.modeledMembers} members generate {money(mature.revenue)}; the capacity limit is {mature.safeMembers} members. The cost basis includes $150,000 associate salaries, employer costs, local operations, provisional coverage and the proposed performance pool. Final profit and complete funding requirements still depend on actual staffing, attendance, collections and coverage costs.</p>
    <section class="concept-detail"><h3>The cost basis remains visible</h3><dl class="cost-list">{costRows.map(([label,value])=><div><dt>{label}</dt><dd>{money(value)}</dd></div>)}<div class="total"><dt>Annual cost before coverage and bonuses</dt><dd>{money(mature.enteredBaseCost)}</dd></div><div><dt>Additional evening/weekend support (allowance)</dt><dd>{money(30000)}</dd></div><div><dt>Additional leave relief (allowance)</dt><dd>{money(20000)}</dd></div><div><dt>Recurring recruiting and development (allowance)</dt><dd>{money(5000)}</dd></div><div><dt>Additional extended-hours operations (allowance)</dt><dd>{money(5000)}</dd></div></dl><p class="note">These are illustrative figures, not payroll disclosures or quotes. Enter zero only when an increment is genuinely absent or already included. Relief expense alone does not create appointment capacity; add its roster too. Compensation is independent of working hours.</p></section>
    <div class="table-region"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>Before / after: each revision’s own operating assumptions, not an isolated schedule experiment</caption><thead><tr><th>Revision</th><th>Modeled members</th><th>Earned revenue</th><th>Expenses incl. bonus</th><th>Operating surplus</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[['R07 published',r07.mature],['R08 draft',r08.mature],['R09 published',mature]].map(([label,m])=><tr><th>{label}</th><td data-label="Modeled members">{m.modeledMembers}</td><td data-label="Earned revenue">{money(m.revenue)}</td><td data-label="Expenses incl. bonus">{money(m.costs)}</td><td data-label="Operating surplus">{money(m.surplus)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <h3>Downside, base and upside remain conditional.</h3>
    <div class="table-region"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>Same prices, salaries and expenses; explicit changes shown in each scenario</caption><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Supported</th><th>Paid members modeled</th><th>Earned revenue</th><th>Surplus after bonus</th></tr></thead><tbody>{snapshot.cases.map(x=><tr><th>{x.label}</th><td data-label="Supported">{x.mature.safeMembers}</td><td data-label="Paid members modeled">{x.mature.modeledMembers}</td><td data-label="Earned revenue">{money(x.mature.revenue)}</td><td data-label="Surplus after bonus">{money(x.mature.surplus)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>The upside depends on both paid enrollment and lower attendance; it does not establish delivery of a monthly habit at 360 members. The owner-heavy case tests 40% owner / 30% A / 30% B demand. Break-even requires {mature.breakEvenMembers} members; the {reference.targetMargin}% target margin requires {mature.targetMembers}, with the reference payment mix and compensation rule.</p>
    <h3>Optional junior stays outside the base.</h3>
    <div class="table-region"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>100% attendance, 50% annual prepay; optional junior includes a new planning panel</caption><thead><tr><th>Roster</th><th>Supported</th><th>Modeled / 360</th><th>Surplus</th></tr></thead><tbody>{snapshot.scheduleComparisons.map(x=><tr><th>{x.label}</th><td data-label="Supported">{x.requested.safeMembers}</td><td data-label="Modeled / 360">{x.requested.modeledMembers}</td><td data-label="Surplus">{money(x.requested.surplus)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>The junior adds $150,000 salary plus employer burden; additional rooms and support remain uncosted. Its new panel allocation is an explicit scenario, not consent to move existing patients. Incremental paid demand and a continuity plan must justify the hire.</p>
    <h3>The annual-payment mix matters.</h3>
    <div class="table-region"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>{mature.modeledMembers} members at the reference mix. Annual-prepay sensitivities use the 20% discount and the same cost basis.</caption><thead><tr><th>Paying annually</th><th>Earned revenue / year</th><th>Bonus / associate</th><th>Practice surplus after bonuses</th></tr></thead><tbody>{snapshot.annualPaymentSensitivities.map(x=><tr><th scope="row">{x.share}%</th><td data-label="Earned revenue / year">{money(x.mature.revenue)}</td><td data-label="Bonus / associate">{money(x.mature.bonusPerAssociate)}</td><td data-label="Practice surplus after bonuses">{money(x.mature.surplus)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>These are conditional illustrations before tax, debt and startup recovery. At 100% annual payment, the sensitivity needs {snapshot.annualPaymentSensitivities[2].mature.targetMembers} members for the proposed margin after incentives. Upfront cash improves timing; it does not erase the discount.</p>
    <section class="concept-detail" id="compensation"><h3>$150,000 base, plus a share of earned practice performance.</h3>
     <p>Each associate starts at a proposed <strong>$150,000 annual base salary</strong>. The owner retains the existing $180,000 cash-pay assumption. Employer costs are modeled separately. Recruiting acceptance, scheduled workload and leave arrangements must be tested together; this is not a salary-market benchmark or an agreed offer.</p>
     <p>The editable starting proposal allocates <strong>20% of positive membership-funded operating surplus</strong> to a shared associate incentive pool, after base salaries, employer costs, local operations, coverage and the support fee. The pool includes employer costs on bonuses. Gross bonuses are split equally between active associates and replacements; the owner and temporary relief roles are excluded. The default surplus hurdle is $0 and the scorecard is assumed fully earned.</p>
     <p>In the base case, including the $60,000 coverage allowance, the employer-cost pool is {money(mature.incentiveCost)}. Gross bonuses total {money(mature.incentiveCash)}, with {money(mature.incentiveBurden)} in employer costs. Each associate receives {money(mature.bonusPerAssociate)} in gross bonus, or {money(150000+mature.bonusPerAssociate)} total cash pay. The practice operating result is {money(mature.surplus)}; a negative amount is a deficit. The 20% pool, $0 hurdle and full quality award are editable planning assumptions, not agreed compensation. Launch awards use each month’s earned operating surplus; the current rule does not wait for accumulated startup losses to recover.</p>
     <p>Agree on a scorecard for timely follow-up, care-plan completion, appropriate testing and patient experience, with case-mix review and independent clinical judgment. Do not reward excluding complex patients, withholding appropriate care or suppressing incident reporting. The worksheet models monthly accrual and payment; actual payout timing, an annual true-up, startup-loss recovery and a minimum cash reserve need explicit contract terms.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="concept-detail" id="ancillary-care"><h3>Optional services should earn trust before margin.</h3>
     <p><strong>Labs:</strong> order for a documented clinical reason, disclose the test price and any collection or handling fee in advance, explain alternatives, and let patients choose an outside provider. No testing quotas, referral payments or per-test physician commissions are assumed.</p>
     <p><strong>Additional care:</strong> consider evidence-based nutrition, metabolic-health follow-up or procedures only where the team is qualified and the work is separately scoped. Price the professional service transparently and do not charge twice for care already included in membership. Product sales and prescriptions do not trigger bonuses. <a href="#source-ethics">AMA ethics context</a></p>
     <p><strong>Peptides:</strong> a broad category, not a blanket business line. FDA-approved peptide medicines and unapproved or compounded products require different assessments. Any proposed therapy needs a product-specific review of evidence, patient suitability, lawful sourcing and current regulatory status. Compounded drugs are not FDA approved. No speculative peptide sales are included. <a href="#source-fda">FDA context</a></p>
     <p>The base case assumes <strong>$0 ancillary revenue and $0 ancillary margin</strong>. Use separate custom rows for verified gross receipts and direct costs; do not treat lab collections as pure profit. Added services also need explicit capacity and staffing allowances. Extra-visit and custom ancillary revenue are excluded from the profit-share basis, while associated costs remain expenses.</p>
    </section>
   </div></section>
   <section id="launch"><div class="reading-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">05 / Enrollment and cash</p><h2>The opening plan must fund a constrained enrollment ramp.</h2>
    <p>The 36-month illustration opens care in month 2, with the owner starting then, Associate A in month 5 and Associate B in month 11. Thirty people seek to join at opening and 25 more per month afterward. These are demand hypotheses. Enrollment accounts for 1% monthly attrition, onboarding work, day-specific capacity and the selected access buffer.</p>
    <p>The model admits {number(launch.rows[1].newMembers)} expected members at opening after checking onboarding and routine demand. Before full staffing, routine applicants wait for their assigned physician. The same fixed planning allocation governs applicants; joining physicians do not take existing patients from another panel. Short-notice access is limited to staffed days during this ramp. Patient willingness to accept that limited launch offer is unproven. Selecting seven-day access from opening can postpone admissions until the roster supports it.</p>
    <p>Unadmitted applicants do not generate revenue. The waitlist assumes no abandonment and is not traction. If a leadership stage or departure leaves existing members beyond physical capacity, the constrained revenue and cash projections become incomplete. The model does not invent patient transfers or drop their care obligations.</p>
    <div class="takeaway"><p><strong>The complete funding need is unknown.</strong> The base case includes the $60,000 annual coverage allowance and produces a cumulative 36-month operating result of {money(launch.totalOperatingResult)}, before startup spending, debt and financing. Sustained monthly operating break-even: {launch.operatingBreakEvenMonth ? `month ${launch.operatingBreakEvenMonth}` : "not reached in 36 months"}. Accumulated operating-loss recovery: {launch.operatingLossRecoveryMonth ? `month ${launch.operatingLossRecoveryMonth}` : "not reached in 36 months"}. Startup spending, opening cash and a reserve remain unspecified, so these figures do not establish startup investment recovery or the funding required.</p></div>
    <p>Enter equipment, deposits, setup costs, opening cash, funding and a target reserve to complete the cash view. Startup spending occurs once. Opening cash and financing are funding sources. A reserve is a desired cash balance, not a second expense.</p>
    <p>Annual members pay at joining and each renewal. Revenue is earned monthly, while modeled cancellations refund unused prepaid months. Unearned dues remain an obligation. Operating break-even, cumulative operating-loss recovery and startup investment cash recovery are separate results.</p>
    <p class="note">Monthly aggregation does not establish daily liquidity. No debt amortization, tax model, investment return or new-location revenue is inferred. The mature stress option shows requested enrollment beyond capacity; launch admissions remain constrained to preserve assigned obligations.</p>
   </div></section>
   <section class="green-band" id="ownership"><div class="reading-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">06 / Development, leadership and succession</p><h2>Leadership time needs a funded reduction in clinical work.</h2>
    <ol class="ownership-steps"><li><strong>Build a practice.</strong> Associates develop patient relationships, clinical cadence and operating judgment while contributing to enrollment and retention.</li><li><strong>Take on leadership.</strong> Agree on management, mentoring and general-manager responsibilities. Reduce clinical days or bookable hours explicitly as those duties grow.</li><li><strong>Prepare a successor.</strong> Budget recruiting and overlapping pay. Protect the trainer's time and give a replacement an explicit initial schedule.</li><li><strong>Evaluate ownership.</strong> Review sustained economics, continuity, clinical judgment, leadership readiness, financing and governance before a buy-in or new location.</li></ol>
    <p>For illustration, adding two management hours per working day for Associate A in month 24, with unchanged salary, lowers the mature snapshot's enrollment limit from {mature.safeMembers} to {snapshot.sensitivities.leadership.mature.safeMembers}. This optional stage is not built into the default reference. The worksheet lets each physician have their own stage dates, days, blocks, responsibilities and pay.</p>
    <p>A break-even contribution alone does not establish readiness. Longer availability does not by itself prove faster clinical development. A replacement can temporarily take staffing above the proposed three-physician steady team; the model makes that overlap and its pay visible.</p>
    <p>A departing physician loses capacity at the original location after the final paid month. No automatic patient transfer or simultaneous capacity at two locations is assumed. A second location needs its own demand, startup funding and opening plan.</p>
    <p><strong>Drew developed this concept.</strong> Whether he builds the support company or helps a physician-led founding team remains undecided. Founder roles, ownership percentages, compensation agreements and partnerships remain open.</p>
   </div></section>
   <section id="platform"><div class="reading-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">07 / Shared support and investment logic</p><h2>The support company must earn its fee through useful work.</h2>
    <p>Proposed services include administrative workflows, scheduling systems, vendor coordination, recruiting support, operating reports and practice-owner development. Practices would retain clinical judgment, responsibility for care quality and patient relationships.</p>
    <p>The reference fee is $5,000 per location per month. Reviewers should compare a costed service package with a realistic independent-practice alternative: what work disappears, what becomes more reliable, and what remains local? The fee is an assumption, not evidence of savings or an approved contract.</p>
    <div class="table-region" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Mature network arithmetic"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>Equally mature practices. This is not a rollout forecast.</caption><thead><tr><th>Locations</th><th>Practice revenue / year</th><th>Platform revenue / year</th></tr></thead><tbody>{[1,25,100].map(n=><tr><th scope="row">{n}</th><td data-label="Practice revenue / year">{money(n*mature.revenue)}</td><td data-label="Platform revenue / year">{money(n*reference.platformFee*12)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>Platform earnings equal fees less delivery costs and central overhead. Those costs remain blank until supplied. Combined practice and platform surplus eliminates the internal fee transfer.</p>
    <p>Physician owners could receive practice distributions after obligations. An investment in the support company would depend on its own earnings, contractual rights and ability to retain practices. The model assumes no exit multiple, investor return or automatic venture-capital fit.</p>
    <p class="note">The legal entity structure, clinical boundaries, fee terms and possible franchise obligations need specialist review. The <a href="#source-tma">TMA background paper</a> and <a href="#source-ftc">FTC guidance</a> identify issues to examine, not approval of this arrangement.</p>
   </div></section>
   <section id="validation"><div class="reading-shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">08 / Validation before expansion</p><h2>The next commitment should follow evidence.</h2>
    <div class="table-region" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Validation plan"><table class="responsive-table"><caption>Focused questions for expert review</caption><thead><tr><th>Hypothesis</th><th>Evidence and reviewer</th><th>Decision informed</th></tr></thead><tbody>{tests.map(([hypothesis,evidence,decision])=><tr><th scope="row">{hypothesis}</th><td data-label="Evidence and reviewer">{evidence}</td><td data-label="Decision informed">{decision}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>A proposed pilot observes 25 opt-in members for 90 days each. Starting targets include 90% retention, visits ending in agreed care plans and routine replies within the selected coverage target. The preserved 24-business-hour worksheet field is a discussion target, not a service promise or a capacity constraint; actual response terms remain unresolved. Routine utilization should remain at or below 85% in the planning model.</p>
    <p>The 15% operating margin and six stable months before another location are proposed decision gates. They require validation alongside clinical access and physician recruitment. No pilot, interview, commitment or outcome is claimed.</p>
    <section class="concept-detail"><h3>Pricing and tax treatment need a separate review</h3><p>The IRS distinguishes HSA contribution eligibility from reimbursement using existing HSA funds. In 2026, its contribution exception includes a $150 monthly limit for an individual arrangement, with a $300 limit when more than one individual is covered. The reimbursement definition has no corresponding fee cap, but service and payment conditions still apply. The proposed individual prices and additional-visit terms need review before making any tax-benefit claim. <a href="#source-irs">IRS Notice 2026-5, questions 11–13 and 20</a></p></section>
   </div></section>
  </div>
  <DpcModel />
  <section class="sources-section"><div class="reading-shell"><p class="eyebrow">Sources and revision notes</p><h2>Public facts and working assumptions remain separate.</h2><ol class="sources">{sources.map(([id,label,note,url])=><li id={`source-${id}`}><a href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{label} ↗</a><p>{note}</p></li>)}</ol><p>Original sources checked September 19, 2026; ancillary-care sources reviewed for R06. All amounts are USD. The reference deck and narrative are dated illustrations. Web inputs change the working model, not the PDF.</p><section class="concept-detail"><h3>R09: continuity panels and protected team access</h3><p>R08 corrected the roster but retained fixed weekday pooled demand, producing a 247-member bottleneck that did not represent the intended care policy. R09 replaces that restriction with assigned physician panels and routine scheduling across each physician’s clinic days. It uses 100% attendance as the base, separates held reserve from completed encounters, and tracks assigned launch panels without silent reassignment.</p><p>R09 is published. R08 remains an unpublished draft superseded by R09. Existing worksheets keep their original inputs, names, notes, feedback and calculation engines. Start R09 assigned-panel proposal explicitly to retain the active worksheet and open the new model. The PDF is a fixed R09 reference.</p></section><a class="text-download" href="/dpc-deck.pdf?v=R09" download="Direct-Primary-Care-Growth-Model.pdf">Download the dated reference deck</a></div></section>
  <section id="revision-history" class="sources-section"><div class="reading-shell"><p class="eyebrow">Publication record</p><h2>Eight published iterations; R08 remains an unpublished draft.</h2><p>Published revision: <strong>{revision.pageRevision}</strong>. Content finalized {updated}; published {centralTimestamp(revision.publishedAt)} after successful deployment. R08 is an unpublished draft superseded by R09 and excluded from the publication count. The content-finalization timestamp stays fixed, separate from publication and the viewer's clock. Central Time uses CDT in daylight time and CST in standard time.</p><p class="note">One revision counts each distinct successful release of this concept's page, calculator or PDF. Multiple commits in a release, failed deployments and unrelated site edits do not count. Earlier names below describe historical versions only and imply no current affiliation.</p><ol class="revision-log">{revision.history.map(entry=><li><h3>{entry.id} / {entry.title}</h3><p><time datetime={entry.date}>{centralTimestamp(entry.date)}</time> — {entry.basis}.</p><p>{entry.summary}</p><a href={entry.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Release evidence ↗</a></li>)}</ol><p class="note">Page {revision.pageRevision}; calculation model {revision.modelVersion}; saved-file schema {revision.schemaVersion}; PDF {revision.pdfRevision}. These identify different things. Publication success is checked against the release workflow separately from the frozen content timestamp.</p></div></section>
